Step-by-Step Guide to Dockerize a Simple Python Flask Web Application
This guide will walk you through creating a simple Python Flask web application, Dockerizing it, and displaying it in a web browser.

Step 1: Set Up Your Project
Create a project directory:



```
mkdir flask-docker-app
cd flask-docker-app
```
Create the required files: Inside the flask-docker-app directory, create the following files:

`app.py (Your Flask application)
requirements.txt (Dependencies for the application)
Dockerfile (To define your Docker image)`


Step 2: Write Your Flask Application
```
Create app.py:
nano app.py
Add the following code:

from flask import Flask

app = Flask(_name_)

@app.route('/')
def home():
    return '<h1>Hello, Dockerized Flask App!</h1>'

if _name_ == '_main_':
    app.run(host='0.0.0.0', port=5000)


```
Step 3: Specify Dependencies

Create and edit requirements.txt:

```
nano requirements.txt
Add Flask to the file:
flask
```

Step 4: Write the Dockerfile

```
# Use an official Python runtime as a parent image
FROM python:3.9-slim-buster

# Set the working directory in the container
WORKDIR /app

# Copy the dependencies file to the working directory
COPY requirements.txt .

# Install any dependencies
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt

# Copy the current directory contents into the container at /app
COPY . .

# Make port 5000 available to the world outside this container
EXPOSE 5000

# Run the application
CMD ["python", "app.py"]
```

Step 5: Build the Docker Image
Run the following command to build the Docker image:

```
sudo docker build -t flask-docker-app .
```

Step 6: Run the Docker Container
Run the container and map the internal port 5000 to the external port 5000:
```
sudo docker run -d -p 5000:5000 flask-docker-app
```

Step 7: Check the Running Container
Verify the container is running:
```
sudo docker ps
```
Step 8: Access the Application in the Browser
If running on your local machine, open your browser and go to:
http://localhost:5000
If running on a remote server (e.g., AWS EC2):

Ensure port 5000 is open in your Security Group (Inbound Rules).

http://<your-ec2-public-ip>:5000
Replace <your-ec2-public-ip> with your instance's public IP.
Expected Output in Browser
When you visit the URL, you should see:

`
Hello, Dockerized Flask App!
`
